WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:09.344
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome back to Superstars in History , the talk show interviewing famous people from the

2
00:00:09.345 --> 00:00:18.211
past. I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was an inventor who really lit up the

3
00:00:18.212 --> 00:00:28.453
world! He held 1,093 patents for his inventions, but you probably know him for his most famous creation, the

4
00:00:28.454 --> 00:00:47.393
lightbulb. Please welcome the very bright Thomas

5
00:00:47.394 --> 00:00:51.676
Edison! THOMAS EDISON: Thank you, Faye. I'm excited to be

6
00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:57.296
here. FAYE MOSS: You were born the youngest of seven children in 1847, in

7
00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:01.377
Ohio. Did your genius shine through as a

8
00:01:01.378 --> 00:01:06.251
child? THOMAS EDISON: Oh, no, I was a pretty poor student in

9
00:01:06.252 --> 00:01:13.218
school. My teachers thought my mind wandered too much, which caused my mother to teach me at home

10
00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:18.458
instead. My mother was one of my biggest supporters; she always believed in

11
00:01:18.459 --> 00:01:22.036
me. FAYE MOSS: She must have been a brilliant

12
00:01:22.037 --> 00:01:32.053
teacher! THOMAS EDISON: My mother actually was a schoolteacher, but I stopped my education when I was 12 to get a job selling newspapers and candy on a

13
00:01:32.054 --> 00:01:34.344
train. The job gave me lots of free

14
00:01:34.345 --> 00:01:40.315
time. Oh, and by then, I was losing my hearing and could only hear a little out of one

15
00:01:40.316 --> 00:01:46.875
ear. I'm not sure what caused it, but I didn't let it interfere with any of my other

16
00:01:46.876 --> 00:01:54.945
interests. For example, in the train's baggage car, I had a printing press, which I used to start my own

17
00:01:54.946 --> 00:01:59.182
newspaper. I even set up a laboratory for my chemistry

18
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:04.605
experiments. But one day a bottle of phosphorous fell and set the baggage car on

19
00:02:04.606 --> 00:02:09.589
fire. The conductor was so mad at me, he threw me off the

20
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.507
train! FAYE MOSS: That wasn't your last train ride

21
00:02:12.508 --> 00:02:18.314
though. In fact, a near accident with a train led to your working with the

22
00:02:18.315 --> 00:02:27.003
telegraph. THOMAS EDISON: Yes, that's true! In 1862 I rescued a three-year-old from a railroad track when a train was about to hit

23
00:02:27.004 --> 00:02:33.440
him. As a reward, the boy's father taught me all about railroad telegraphs and Morse

24
00:02:33.441 --> 00:02:37.938
code. I was able to use those skills to get work as a telegraph

25
00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:41.384
operator. This work let me travel around the

26
00:02:41.385 --> 00:02:50.855
U.S. But I always knew what I really wanted to do was invent things, so I started inventing on the

27
00:02:50.856 --> 00:02:54.155
side. FAYE MOSS: What was your first major

28
00:02:54.156 --> 00:03:01.456
invention? THOMAS EDISON: In June 1869 I received my very first patent for an electric vote

29
00:03:01.457 --> 00:03:09.051
recorder. Even though politicians didn't want to use my machine, I wasn't too discouraged, at least not at

30
00:03:09.052 --> 00:03:12.870
first. Inventing was fun—maybe too much

31
00:03:12.871 --> 00:03:18.400
fun! In 1871 I married my first wife, Mary

32
00:03:18.401 --> 00:03:25.501
Stilwell. We had three children, two of whom I nicknamed Dot and Dash after the telegraphic

33
00:03:25.502 --> 00:03:32.334
terms. Mary was often ill and unhappy about how much time I spent on my

34
00:03:32.335 --> 00:03:36.724
inventions. She needed me to devote more hours to taking care of

35
00:03:36.725 --> 00:03:43.848
her. Eventually I decided that I wouldn't waste time inventing things that no one

36
00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:46.313
wanted. FAYE MOSS: What did people

37
00:03:46.314 --> 00:03:48.368
want? THOMAS EDISON: A better

38
00:03:48.369 --> 00:03:57.727
telegraph! Like some other impressive inventors at the time, including Alexander Graham Bell, I thought I could make the machine faster and

39
00:03:57.728 --> 00:04:01.494
better. And in 1874 I had a

40
00:04:01.495 --> 00:04:09.506
breakthrough! My quadruplex telegraph let people send four signals on a single wire at the same

41
00:04:09.507 --> 00:04:16.305
time. My invention was so successful, I was able to buy a new laboratory in Menlo Park, New

42
00:04:16.306 --> 00:04:20.112
Jersey. It became known as the Invention

43
00:04:20.113 --> 00:04:25.557
Factory. FAYE MOSS: You and Alexander Graham Bell seem to have had similar interests.

44
00:04:25.558 --> 00:04:33.041
THOMAS EDISON: I always felt like I was competing with him, since we both wanted to improve sound and communication.

45
00:04:33.042 --> 00:04:40.469
When he said he'd invented a device to transmit the human voice, I wanted to make an even better one!

46
00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:49.794
I ended up creating a transmitter that let people use the telephone over much greater distances than Bell's original design.

47
00:04:49.795 --> 00:04:54.870
FAYE MOSS: The telephone also inspired another invention of yours, didn't it?

48
00:04:54.871 --> 00:05:03.567
THOMAS EDISON: Yes! I wanted to record telephone messages mechanically, so I came up with the phonograph!

49
00:05:03.568 --> 00:05:12.337
When sound traveled through the machine, the vibrations caused a needle to make indentations on a cylinder.

50
00:05:12.338 --> 00:05:17.425
Then the cylinder could play back a recording.

51
00:05:17.426 --> 00:05:29.651
To test the device, I spoke the words "Mary had a little lamb" into the mouthpiece, and it played the phrase back to me.

52
00:05:29.652 --> 00:05:34.815
Newspapers and critics were amazed, and I became quite famous.

53
00:05:34.816 --> 00:05:40.586
Some even called me the Wizard of Menlo Park after that.

54
00:05:40.587 --> 00:05:48.435
FAYE MOSS: The phonograph wasn't your last act of wizardry. Tell us about inventing the lightbulb!

55
00:05:48.436 --> 00:05:52.699
THOMAS EDISON: I actually wasn't the first to make a lightbulb.

56
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:58.184
However, most of the lightbulbs that had been created only stayed lit for a few minutes.

57
00:05:58.185 --> 00:06:02.048
Or they were too expensive to be widely used.

58
00:06:02.049 --> 00:06:09.711
In 1878 I made my own lightbulb with help from a few friends, and I worked on perfecting it.

59
00:06:09.712 --> 00:06:17.276
By the next year, I had made one that lasted 13.5 hours.

60
00:06:17.277 --> 00:06:25.101
I went on to create bulbs that lasted even longer—up to 1,200 hours!

61
00:06:25.102 --> 00:06:31.259
FAYE MOSS: So if the lightbulb already existed, why did you get so much credit for it?

62
00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:40.157
THOMAS EDISON: Well, mine was the first that was both efficient and affordable, which allowed electric lightbulbs to be widely used.

63
00:06:40.158 --> 00:06:48.667
Thanks to my invention, the first lighting system that could support a whole city was installed in New York City.

64
00:06:48.668 --> 00:06:56.352
Soon cities across the world, like Paris, London, and Moscow, lit up!

65
00:06:56.353 --> 00:07:05.945
I also had my own electric company, which attracted young, talented inventors and entrepreneurs such as Nikola Tesla and Henry Ford.

66
00:07:05.946 --> 00:07:09.798
FAYE MOSS: Did you have any other popular inventions?

67
00:07:09.799 --> 00:07:16.016
THOMAS EDISON: I went on to invent many other things, but not all were successful.

68
00:07:16.017 --> 00:07:22.549
One of my favorites was a motion picture device I worked on in the 1890s.

69
00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:35.561
This device was called the Kinetoscope. Places opened in major U.S. cities so people could watch silent, very short films on the machines.

70
00:07:35.562 --> 00:07:44.454
Eventually one of my assistants and I were able to create another device—one that could project pictures and make sound!

71
00:07:44.455 --> 00:07:51.922
FAYE MOSS: Your phonograph, lightbulb, and Kinetoscope definitely had a big influence on the world.

72
00:07:51.923 --> 00:07:56.098
Thanks so much for sharing your genius with us today!

73
00:07:56.099 --> 00:08:06.220
THOMAS EDISON: Genius is 1 percent inspiration and 99 percent hard work! Good-bye!

74
00:08:06.221 --> 00:08:10.344
FAYE MOSS: Thomas Edison never lost his passion for inventing.

75
00:08:10.345 --> 00:08:18.974
He developed processes to build houses with cement and did research and experiments for the U.S. Navy during World War I.

76
00:08:18.975 --> 00:08:24.189
Even in his seventies, he continued to work and stay active.

77
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:28.877
He died on October 18, 1931.

78
00:08:28.878 --> 00:08:39.407
President Hoover asked everyone to turn off all electric lights across the country on the evening of Edison's funeral to remember his accomplishments.

79
00:08:39.408 --> 00:08:46.257
Edison's inventions certainly lit up the world and made our lives a lot brighter.

80
00:08:46.258 --> 00:08:49.202
Thanks, everyone, for watching today.

81
00:08:49.203 --> 00:08:56.923
I'll be back again soon with another famous guest from history! Good-bye!

